  {
   "id": "YlfywE_YAgAJ",
   "from": "Daniel Turek",
   "author": "c9e9cbbfb3b043f0",
   "date": "2020-06-03T14:53:20Z",
   "subject": "Re: A strange error with ST models",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">This is coming up when it tries to calculate the posterior &quot;summary&quot;, from your request &quot;summary =  TRUE&quot;, which includes the 95% BCIs for all parameters monitored, which uses the R quantile() function.  When quantile() is called on a vector containing one or more NaN values, that&#39;s the error you get.  Somewhere in the course of MCMC sampling, a NaN value was generated for at least one of the parameter sample chains.  Without more information about the model, initial values, and the MCMC samplers, it&#39;s impossible to diagnose more.  I&#39;d suggest you look at the samples, too, which should also have been returned (samples$samples, I believe), and look at which parameter(s) are giving the NaN samples, and continue to investigate from there.  Bad initial values, or essentially &quot;improper model initialization&quot; is a common source of problems like this.<div><br></div><div>Keep at it.</div><div><br></div><div>Daniel</div><div><br></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Wed, Jun 3, 2020 at 10:48 AM Lawson, Andrew B. &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">laws...@musc.edu</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
